    {
      "id": "rjGmTejRUb679EqWbwUTe",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 525.8888888888887,
      "y": 542.4722222222222,
      "width": 708.6666666666667,
      "height": 56.76982591876204,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1067702084,
      "version": 199,
      "versionNonce": 34812228,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793035547856,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Subqueries in SQL\nA subquery is a query inside another SQL query.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Subqueries in SQL\nA subquery is a query inside another SQL query.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "I0BSu-fqkvp-P4CVx7Bjg",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1536.7724967778709,
      "y": 562.4722222222224,
      "width": 1822.7127415069695,
      "height": 85.15473887814306,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3G",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1921920508,
      "version": 576,
      "versionNonce": 636347388,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793039342677,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What is GROUP BY in SQL?\nGROUP BY groups rows that have the same value in a particular column, allowing us to perform calculations on each group using functions like\nCOUNT(), SUM(), AVG(), MAX(), and MIN().",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What is GROUP BY in SQL?\nGROUP BY groups rows that have the same value in a particular column, allowing us to perform calculations on each group using functions like COUNT(), SUM(), AVG(), MAX(), and MIN().",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "kt9dfVd2FOUZSzojwlq_s",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1551.8458558083216,
      "y": 1338.1890725199914,
      "width": 1832.050196520945,
      "height": 198.69439071566714,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3I",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 739239620,
      "version": 717,
      "versionNonce": 1309040324,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793039339076,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "GROUP_CONCAT() in SQL\n\nGROUP_CONCAT() is an aggregate function in MySQL that combines multiple values from different rows into a single string, separated by commas\nby default. It is particularly useful when working with many-to-many relationships. For example, a book can belong to multiple categories,\nand a category can contain multiple books. To represent this relationship, we use a junction table containing book_id and category_id. When\nwe join the three tables, a book appears multiple times if it belongs to multiple categories. Using GROUP_CONCAT(), we can combine all the\ncategory names into a single column, so each book appears only once in the final result.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "GROUP_CONCAT() in SQL\n\nGROUP_CONCAT() is an aggregate function in MySQL that combines multiple values from different rows into a single string, separated by commas by default. It is particularly useful when working with many-to-many relationships. For example, a book can belong to multiple categories, and a category can contain multiple books. To represent this relationship, we use a junction table containing book_id and category_id. When we join the three tables, a book appears multiple times if it belongs to multiple categories. Using GROUP_CONCAT(), we can combine all the category names into a single column, so each book appears only once in the final result.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "PGDYdc4vAbM7FIP8gmmGJ",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -931.1111111111113,
      "y": -1662.5277777777776,
      "width": 4208.666666666667,
      "height": 56.76982591876204,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3K",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1047995004,
      "version": 556,
      "versionNonce": 2040569084,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793037274031,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What is GROUP BY in SQL?\nGROUP BY groups rows that have the same value in a particular column, allowing us to perform calculations on each group using functions like COUNT(), SUM(), AVG(), MAX(), and MIN().",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What is GROUP BY in SQL?\nGROUP BY groups rows that have the same value in a particular column, allowing us to perform calculations on each group using functions like COUNT(), SUM(), AVG(), MAX(), and MIN().",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "s2eQSMV8kogbfIKkuJyRq",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2035.8888888888887,
      "y": 552.4722222222224,
      "width": 1208.6666666666667,
      "height": 170.30947775628613,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3O",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1383456708,
      "version": 523,
      "versionNonce": 1330596036,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793035917019,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What are CTEs in SQL?\nCTE (Common Table Expression) is a temporary, named result set that you define using the\nWITH keyword and use inside a SQL query.\n\nThink of it as creating a temporary result with a name so you can write complex queries in\nsmaller, more readable steps.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What are CTEs in SQL?\nCTE (Common Table Expression) is a temporary, named result set that you define using the WITH keyword and use inside a SQL query.\n\nThink of it as creating a temporary result with a name so you can write complex queries in smaller, more readable steps.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "OlgYEe93s22_f8GLQEHHB",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 531.8888888888887,
      "y": 1324.4722222222222,
      "width": 918.6666666666667,
      "height": 56.76982591876204,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a3V",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1086332412,
      "version": 368,
      "versionNonce": 220494276,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1793035612146,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "2. Correlated Subqueries in SQL\nA correlated subquery is a subquery that depends on the outer query.",
      "fontSize": 22.707930367504815,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "2. Correlated Subqueries in SQL\nA correlated subquery is a subquery that depends on the outer query.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
